{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788365445,"nonce":"xPN7vf0NOU3Cs0aG1WMztELnbAo=","message":"**Built on `feat/df378fa-cockpit`.** `cockpit/` at repo root, peer to `rwa/` and\n`accept/`: the cockpit is neither a protocol client nor a grader, it is a third\nconsumer of the same on-disk artifacts, so it gets its own directory rather than\nliving inside either. `cockpit/cockpit` is the entry point, mirroring `rwa/rwa`.\n\n**Item 2, the real deliverable — measured, not asserted.**\n`cockpit/verify-readonly.py` snapshots the protocol root, runs the app headless\nin `--follow` for 60s under a `sys.addaudithook` that fails on any write-mode\n`open`, socket, subprocess, unlink, rename or mkdir, then diffs. Against the\nreal `_RimWorld-Agent` protocol root (15,460 files):\n\n```\ncommands/ + results/ gained   0 file(s)   PASS\nnew transcript step dirs      PASS (0)\nnon-journal files that grew   0   PASS\naudit hook violations         0   PASS\n```\n\nThe two halves are independent on purpose. The directory diff is\nbench-specific and a third party writing during the window would confound it;\nthe audit hook is not, and proves the PROCESS wrote nothing regardless.\n\n**The watermark cannot be measured, and that is the point.**\n`readWatermark` is a private static in `Journal.cs`, never persisted, so it\ncannot be read off disk — and the only thing that returns it is the `journal`\nverb, whose call MOVES it. The observation is destructive, which is exactly why\nthis tool does not make it. Proved statically instead, and the chain has no\nbranches: `readWatermark` is moved only by `Journal.NoteRead`; `NoteRead`'s only\ncaller is `JournalVerbs.Read`; that runs only when `Poller.ScanInbox` finds a\nfile in `commands/`. **`commands/` gained no file, therefore the watermark did\nnot move.** That is a stronger claim than reading the number would have been.\n\n**Item 3 needed more than the link walk.** Following `meta.json` alone shows\n**999 of 4,599 steps — 21.7%**, because m1-20260901's five segments predate\n5eba561 and carry no `prev`/`next`/`base`/`segment` at all. `rwa` derives those\nlinks the first time it opens the run (`Transcript.link_chain`, gated on\n`legacy`) — which is a WRITE, into a directory a driver may be appending to. So\nthe cockpit derives siblings by name instead, using rwa's own rule\n(`Transcript.segments`), anchored on `-s` + digits so a run named `m1` cannot\nswallow one named `m1-retry`. `--probe` resolves all five.\n\n**Item 6 degrades honestly, and a second shape turned up that also had to.**\nThe three steps with `cmd.json` and no `result.json` (`125-digest`,\n`324-bill-options`, `-s02/574-consume`) render as NEVER RETURNED with the\ncommand that was asked for. The second shape: **the tick column is not\nmonotonic — the run steps backwards 67 times.** `-s00/521-advance` ends at tick\n2,276,023 and `522-pawn` reports 2,223,181, because a step directory is claimed\nwhen the command is SENT and two clients against one bench interleave. Handled\nhere (day anchors take a strict maximum, 68 rather than 92; the journal delta is\nkeyed on the order results actually returned so no line lands on two steps; the\nsidebar says \"returned BEFORE the previous step\"), and **filed as `3d53df2`**,\nbecause `accept/4.2-play-loop.py`'s `advance_discipline` reasons about adjacency\nand 78 of the run's 489 advances have a next step whose result returned earlier.\n\n**A design decision worth recording: the map is sticky.** The run makes TEN\n`map-view` calls in 4,599 steps — nine before the first advance, one on the last\nday. A hero panel that blanked for the other 4,589 would hide exactly what\n`next-run.md` §C is about, so it shows the last map at or before the step, with\nthe border title reading `map · 2,487 steps ago` and the caption naming the\nstep it came from. The neglect becomes the most visible thing on screen.\n\n**Dorian expanded the scope mid-build** — *\"it's also to see if your tools and\nvision make sense … I need to see everything you do, even the actions you could\ntake, files you're posed to read, memories\"* — so there is a second page (`tab`).\nIt renders the mod's whole `[Verb(\"…\")]` registry (read from Source; asking the\nbench would mean sending a command) against the ops the run reached, with the\naccent marking what the agent had used by THIS step, so scrubbing lights the\nsurface up as the run learns its own tools. Beside it the shelf it was meant to\nbe reading — playbook, checklists, colony memory, the run's own documents — and\n`checklist.ndjson`, the one place it recorded a reading and a note rather than a\nverb call. On m1-20260901: **133 verbs, 71 used, 62 never called**, the whole\n`dev:` god-hand dark (correct) and `quest*`, `policy*`, `orders`, `repair`,\n`schedule` dark (a finding).\n\n**Not demonstrated.** No live, actively-driven bench: RimWorld was not running\nat any point during this work and I am not permitted to launch it, so the 60s\ncheck ran against a real protocol root with a four-hour-stale `status.json` and\nnothing writing into it. Follow mode was proved instead against a directory\ngrown by hand — step dirs copied in one at a time, crossing a segment rotation\nmid-flight and ending on a step with no `result.json`: the cursor tracked the\ntail, the new segment was picked up, the in-flight step rendered as such,\nscrubbing dropped follow and `f` restored it. That script was not committed\n(the coordinator's instruction was one short check, not a suite); it is the one\npiece of evidence here that a reviewer cannot re-run from the repo.\n\nAlso not demonstrated: any rendering in a real terminal emulator. Every capture\nis Textual's headless driver.","files":null}]}